result.core_idea:
No node in the memory plane owns a trained embedding. Every vector above the frozen leaf vectors comes from a few small encoders that every node shares: an up-aggregator, a sibling/lateral encoder, a down-contextualiser, and the CLM-style bridge heads. They are applied to whatever the graph currently is, so the graph is an input to fixed functions, as in GraphSAGE and TGN. When a node is created, merged, split or rewritten, one forward pass gives it its vectors. When a node is forgotten, its vectors stop being computed. No retraining is needed in either case. Vectors are a cache keyed by Merkle hashes (child hashes, gist hash, weight version), so a change recomputes only its own path and a bounded neighbourhood, and a deletion leaves nothing derived from the deleted item. Three separate clocks keep "dynamic embeddings" trainable and testable:
- the data clock: every write; recompute only;
- the feedback clock: per-node state built from an event log, nightly, and can be replayed;
- the weight clock: offline training on public or synthetic episodes with full recompute; each versioned release triggers a cheap re-derivation, because the expensive leaf encoder never changes.
Parent keys hold several slot vectors, scored by max, because the mean of the children measured 0.386. The tree only ranks and never hides anything: flat dense search plus BM25 over the leaves stays the recall floor.

risks:
Deletion leaks outside the vector maths:
- LLM gists paraphrase deleted content into ancestors (regeneration must cover the whole chain);
- undo payloads and old gists in the op log;
- SQLite free pages, nearest-neighbour index tombstones, backups;
- logs that stored a vector or an ask.
The guarantee holds only if derived vectors are never persisted outside the cache and W never trains on device.

result.minimal_experiment:
Proposed script: /home/user/Tessera/scripts/ai/small_models/e8_inductive_pyramid.py. It reuses load, train, bm25_rank and fuse from e5_bridges.py, runs on the pinned locomo10.json (sha 79fa87e9...), is CPU only, and has no API call.

Graph per chat:
- leaves = turns (c_x from bge-small, cached to .npy);
- level 1 = LoCoMo sessions (time axis, exact);
- root = the chat;
- lateral edges = adjacent sessions only;
- no topic axis, and no learned ops.
Model: E5's identity + rank-16 bridges, plus:
- up-encoder: k-means seeds with m=4, low-rank 4-head attention, gamma initialised to 0;
- leaf contextualiser: input [c_x ; u_session ; ctx ; tau], gate initialised to 0;
- lateral encoder: one sibling round.
Training:
- 5 folds as in E5 (2 held-out chats each), 40 epochs;
- loss L_leaf + 0.5 L_route + 0.1 L_stab;
- two augmented views per chat (merge adjacent sessions p=0.3, split sessions over 20 turns p=0.3, drop 10% of non-evidence turns);
- full recompute every step.

Arms (recall@10 over the about 1,531 questions, paired bootstrap 95% CIs against A2, per-category breakdown):
- A0 BM25: must reproduce 0.563.
- A1 frozen: 0.575.
- A2 E5 low-rank bridged: about 0.657.
- B1 sessions-first, mean pooling: about 0.386.
- B2 sessions-first, BM25 choice: about 0.649.
- B3 sessions-first, k-means slots, untrained (gamma=0).
- B4 sessions-first, learned slots.
- C1 contextual keys a_x, flat.
- C2 C1 + BM25 by RRF.
- C3 C2 + tree prior (lambda * log p(session|q)).
Also report evidence-session recall@3 for the B arms.

Dynamic protocol, the core claim. Weights are trained once and never touched afterwards. On held-out chats evaluate:
- (S) static true sessions;
- (I) incremental: sessions streamed one at a time with cached updates; questions asked at the end;
- (I-early) each question asked right after its last evidence session arrives, against an A2 baseline over the same leaves at that moment;
- (P) perturbed at test time: random merges, splits and deletion of 10% of non-evidence turns, 3 seeds;
- (U) a random op sequence followed by its inverse.

Checks:
- incremental cache vs full recompute: max |diff| < 1e-5;
- U returns hashes and vectors identical to S;
- forget(x) for 20 random leaves: every vector equals a full recompute on G without x, and no array holds x's row.

Pass lines, written before running:
- P1: B4 >= 0.649 and B3 > 0.45. Learned or multi-slot parents fix the mean collapse.
- P2: max(C1, C2, C3) - A2 >= +0.010, with the CI above 0.
- P3: |P - S| <= 0.010 and |I - S| <= 0.005, with weights unchanged. This is the inductive claim.
- P4: all exactness, undo and deletion checks pass.

Reading the outcome:
- P3 and P4 pass but P2 fails: the dynamic machinery is sound, but context does not help on LoCoMo. Keep the tree for packing and gists only, and write that up.
- P1 fails: the tree must not filter, and BM25 session choice stays.

Budget: encoding about 7.4k texts with bge-small on CPU takes about 1-3 min. Training is about 5 folds x 40 epochs x 8 chats x 2 views at about 20-40 ms each, about 1-2 min. Evaluation with 3 perturbation seeds and the streaming arms is about 2-4 min. Total about 10 min, under 20.

Not tested here: learned merge/split heads, the topic and entity axes, gist vectors, and the event memory m_v. Each needs its own later run.

risks:
Deletion leaks outside the vector maths:
- paraphrase in ancestor gists, so whole-gist regeneration plus a leak check is required;
- undo payloads; FTS5 segments; SQLite free pages and the WAL; backups;
- Spotlight and App Intents indexes;
- reasoning-plane transcripts and prompt caches, which the app's own deletion must cover.

design.minimal_experiment:
e8_pyramid_encoder.py on the pinned LoCoMo10, CPU only, no API call, about 10-15 minutes. Full spec in prototype_spec.

Setup:
- Leaves are turns; parents are LoCoMo sessions; a root exists only to test path propagation.
- One shared up-encoder (k-means seeds + one low-rank attention block, zero-init output) is trained jointly with E5's rank-16 bridges, by InfoNCE at the leaf and session levels.
- 5 folds of 2 held-out chats.

Arms:
- reference: the reproduced baselines (BM25 0.563, frozen 0.575, low-rank bridges 0.657, BM25 sessions 0.649, mean sessions 0.386), plus flat bridged+BM25;
- a de-confounded parent comparison with the inside ranker held fixed: BM25, mean, untrained k-means slots, exact log-sum-exp, learned slots;
- top-down reads fused with the flat bridge.

Dynamic tests, with no retraining:
- a streaming build equals the batch build bit for bit;
- forgetting 20% of leaves: forgotten leaves never rank, parents change and equal a fresh build;
- merging adjacent sessions, at fixed parent count and at a matched leaf budget, with undo round-trip;
- a weight update by reseed: a timed full recompute with zero encoder calls and refusal of mixed versions.

Results: recall@10, multi-hop recall@10, parents opened, and paired-bootstrap CIs against the leaf-only bridges.

Pass lines are fixed before the run (see prototype_spec).
